<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Allendowney.github.io on Chege's Blog</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/domains/allendowney.github.io/</link><description>Recent content in Allendowney.github.io on Chege's Blog</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Sat, 03 Oct 2026 23:38:23 -0700</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.curiosities.dev/domains/allendowney.github.io/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Probability</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/mathematics/probability_and_stochastic_systems/sample_space_and_probability/01-probability/</link><pubDate>Thu, 17 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.curiosities.dev/mathematics/probability_and_stochastic_systems/sample_space_and_probability/01-probability/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="ingredients-of-probability"&gt;Ingredients of Probability&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There is an outcome space, \(\Omega\), e.g., the number of students in the
first class: \( \Omega = \{ 0, 1, 2, 3, &amp;hellip; \} \). &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#ORF309"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are events of interest, e.g., at least one student shows up: \(A = \{1,
2, 3, &amp;hellip; \} \). &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#ORF309"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A probability measure, \(\mathbb{P}\), takes events \(A\) and assigns them
\(\mathbb{P}(A) \in [0, 1]\), such that the axioms of probability are
satisfied. &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#ORF309"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Conditional Probability</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/mathematics/probability_and_stochastic_systems/sample_space_and_probability/04-conditional-probability/</link><pubDate>Fri, 29 Sep 2017 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.curiosities.dev/mathematics/probability_and_stochastic_systems/sample_space_and_probability/04-conditional-probability/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="definition"&gt;Definition&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;\( \mathbb{P}(A|B) \) is the probability of event \(A\) given that event
\(B\) occurs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;$$ \mathbb{P}(A|B) = \frac{\mathbb{P}(A \cap B)}{\mathbb{P}(B)} $$&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Notice that if \(P(B) = 0\) then it&amp;rsquo;s meaningless to condition \(A\) on
\(B\). &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#ORF309"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;


